      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the single percent discount that equates to the combined effect of two successive discounts, we'll start by calculating the sale price of the shirt after each discount step-by-step and then determine the effective discount.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the sale price after the first discount\nThe original price of the shirt is $30. The first discount is 20%.\n\n\\[\n\\text{First discount amount} = 30 \\times \\frac{20}{100} = 30 \\times 0.20 = 6\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{Sale price after the first discount} = 30 - 6 = 24\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the sale price after the second discount\nThe sale price after the first discount is $24. The second discount is 25%.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Second discount amount} = 24 \\times \\frac{25}{100} = 24 \\times 0.25 = 6\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{Sale price after the second discount} = 24 - 6 = 18\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Determine the final price after both discounts\nThe final price of the shirt after both discounts is $18.\n\n### Step 4: Calculate the single percent discount\nLet \\( D \\) be the single percent discount needed to reach the final price of $18 from the original price of $30.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Final price after single discount} = 30 \\times \\left(1 - \\frac{D}{100}\\right) = 18\n\\]\n\nWe set up the equation:\n\n\\[\n30 \\times \\left(1 - \\frac{D}{100}\\right) = 18\n\\]\n\n### Step 5: Solve for \\( D \\)\n\\[\n30 - \\frac{30D}{100} = 18\n\\]\n\n\\[\n30 - 0.3D = 18\n\\]\n\n\\[\n30 - 18 = 0.3D\n\\]\n\n\\[\n12 = 0.3D\n\\]\n\n\\[\nD = \\frac{12}{0.3} = 40\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the single percent discount needed to achieve the same final price as the two successive discounts is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{40}\n\\]",
