      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the final price and the corresponding single discount, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the sale price after the 20% off discount.\n2. Calculate the price after applying the 25% off coupon to the sale price.\n3. Determine the single discount that would be equivalent to both discounts combined.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the sale price after the 20% off discount.**\n\nThe regular price of the shirt is $30. A 20% discount means the shirt is sold at 80% of its original price.\n\\[\n\\text{Sale price} = 30 \\times (1 - 0.20) = 30 \\times 0.80 = 24\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the price after applying the 25% off coupon to the sale price.**\n\nThe sale price is $24. A 25% discount on $24 means the shirt is sold at 75% of its sale price.\n\\[\n\\text{Price after coupon} = 24 \\times (1 - 0.25) = 24 \\times 0.75 = 18\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Determine the single discount that would be equivalent to both discounts combined.**\n\nThe final price after both discounts is $18. Let \\( x \\) be the single discount that would give the final price.\n\nThe single discount \\( x \\) means that the shirt is sold at \\((100\\% - x)\\%\\) of the original price. Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n18 = 30 \\times (1 - x)\n\\]\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[\n18 = 30 - 30x\n\\]\n\\[\n30x = 30 - 18\n\\]\n\\[\n30x = 12\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{12}{30} = \\frac{2}{5} = 0.40 = 40\\%\n\\]\n\nThus, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
